1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,004
One fifth of the world's oil and gas used to pass through one small sea passage.

2
00:00:06,004 --> 00:00:11,629
At its narrowest it is only about 39 kilometres wide.

3
00:00:11,629 --> 00:00:15,926
Each shipping lane is less than 4 kilometres wide.

4
00:00:15,926 --> 00:00:18,328
It is called the Strait of Hormuz.

5
00:00:18,328 --> 00:00:24,963
Today oil tankers are burning there, and the heat is reaching our petrol pumps and our kitchens.

6
00:00:24,963 --> 00:00:33,100
By the end of this video you will clearly understand how this hits your pocket, and what could happen next.

7
00:00:37,267 --> 00:00:39,583
Hormuz lies between Iran and Oman.

8
00:00:39,583 --> 00:00:47,763
Saudi Arabia, Iraq, the UAE, Kuwait, Qatar: this is the main door to the open sea for their oil and gas.

9
00:00:47,763 --> 00:00:54,423
In 2025, about 15 million barrels of crude oil passed through it every day.

10
00:00:54,423 --> 00:00:59,491
Most of it goes to Asia: China, India, Japan and Korea.

11
00:00:59,491 --> 00:01:04,207
So if this door shuts, we are among the first to feel it.

12
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:10,396
At the end of February 2026, the US and Israel attacked Iran.

13
00:01:10,396 --> 00:01:14,343
In response, Iran restricted shipping through Hormuz.

14
00:01:14,343 --> 00:01:21,593
For about nine months since, the Gulf has been on a knife edge. Talks and strikes keep taking turns.

15
00:01:21,593 --> 00:01:28,124
This week too, tankers caught fire in Hormuz. Iran says they hit sea mines.

16
00:01:28,124 --> 00:01:35,387
By the UN's count, at least 25 seafarers have been killed in the area since February.

17
00:01:35,533 --> 00:01:39,621
This week the International Energy Agency, the IEA, said something huge.

18
00:01:39,621 --> 00:01:43,520
This is the biggest disruption to oil and gas trade in history.

19
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:47,419
So far, 2.8 billion barrels of Gulf oil have been lost.

20
00:01:47,419 --> 00:01:56,097
During the 1979 Iranian revolution, about 1 billion barrels were lost. So this is almost three times bigger.

21
00:01:56,097 --> 00:02:01,993
This month, crude flow through Hormuz is only about half of pre-war levels.

22
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:09,405
In the first weeks of the war, the world was genuinely scared.

23
00:02:09,405 --> 00:02:16,137
In March, IEA member countries agreed to release 400 million barrels of oil from emergency stocks.

24
00:02:16,137 --> 00:02:22,743
The IEA even warned that Europe had only about six weeks of jet fuel left.

25
00:02:22,743 --> 00:02:26,833
Trade in fertilisers, helium and petrochemicals was disrupted too.

26
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:30,593
When one sea lane closed, the whole world shook together.

27
00:02:30,733 --> 00:02:32,049
Now look at the oil price.

28
00:02:32,049 --> 00:02:36,875
At the end of January, Brent crude was about $71 a barrel.

29
00:02:36,875 --> 00:02:40,134
In October it went above $117.

30
00:02:40,134 --> 00:02:45,523
Now it is about $93, 21% below the peak.

31
00:02:45,523 --> 00:02:48,720
So why is the price falling even while tankers burn?

32
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:55,913
Gulf oil has started flowing again by other routes, and the IEA expects a world oil surplus this quarter.

33
00:02:56,067 --> 00:03:01,324
India imports about 88% of the crude oil it needs.

34
00:03:01,324 --> 00:03:05,579
Before the war, about half of that came through Hormuz.

35
00:03:05,579 --> 00:03:14,904
India switched fast, buying from more than 40 countries including Russia. Now about 70% of its crude avoids Hormuz.

36
00:03:14,904 --> 00:03:17,407
But cooking gas is a different story.

37
00:03:17,407 --> 00:03:25,543
About 60% of the LPG we use is imported, and normally up to 90% of those imports come through Hormuz.

38
00:03:25,543 --> 00:03:28,847
And India has no strategic reserve of LPG.

39
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,614
Now, your pocket.

40
00:03:34,614 --> 00:03:39,844
A year ago, a litre of petrol in Kochi cost about ₹106.

41
00:03:39,844 --> 00:03:44,363
Now it is about ₹114, roughly ₹8 more per litre.

42
00:03:44,363 --> 00:03:50,109
For a car that fills 40 litres a month, that is about ₹320 extra every month.

43
00:03:50,109 --> 00:03:52,756
About ₹3,800 a year.

44
00:03:52,756 --> 00:03:59,060
Diesel went up too, and through freight costs it can reach the price of many things, from vegetables to rice.

45
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:06,987
Next, the 14.2 kg kitchen gas cylinder.

46
00:04:06,987 --> 00:04:12,203
It went up ₹60 in March and ₹29 in June, ₹89 in total.

47
00:04:12,203 --> 00:04:17,791
In Delhi, ₹853 became ₹942.

48
00:04:17,791 --> 00:04:25,862
The 19 kg commercial cylinder used by hotels has gone up for the third month in a row, and this can show up in restaurant prices.

49
00:04:25,862 --> 00:04:32,443
By government figures in June, oil companies were losing about ₹700 on every cylinder.

50
00:04:32,443 --> 00:04:36,220
So pressure for further increases remains.

51
00:04:36,367 --> 00:04:38,689
India pays for oil in dollars.

52
00:04:38,689 --> 00:04:42,496
When oil costs more, India needs more dollars, and the rupee weakens.

53
00:04:42,496 --> 00:04:48,431
In May the rupee fell to a record low of almost 97 to the dollar.

54
00:04:48,431 --> 00:04:53,980
With oil cheaper, it has recovered to about 93.3.

55
00:04:53,980 --> 00:05:02,560
To control inflation, the RBI raised the repo rate to 5.5% in October. Its next meeting is 2 to 4 December.

56
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:06,667
This can also affect EMIs on floating-rate loans.

57
00:05:06,833 --> 00:05:09,570
Can't oil simply avoid Hormuz?

58
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:18,852
In the Gulf, only Saudi Arabia and the UAE have pipelines that bypass Hormuz, and they can carry only part of the normal flow.

59
00:05:18,852 --> 00:05:26,825
Even counting pipelines, Middle East oil exports this month are only 64% of pre-war levels.

60
00:05:26,825 --> 00:05:29,860
Qatar's gas ships have no other way out.

61
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:35,693
That is why these 39 kilometres act like a knot around the world's neck.

62
00:05:35,833 --> 00:05:42,431
What could happen in the coming months? Nobody can say for sure, but let's look at three possibilities.

63
00:05:42,431 --> 00:05:51,384
One: if talks succeed and the sea route opens, oil could fall further. But more than 40 damaged energy sites will take time to repair.

64
00:05:51,384 --> 00:05:57,173
Two: if attacks continue on and off like now, prices will keep swinging.

65
00:05:57,173 --> 00:06:04,511
Three: if the war escalates again, a spike above $110 like October could return.

66
00:06:04,511 --> 00:06:09,156
A new UN resolution on Hormuz is also being negotiated.

67
00:06:09,156 --> 00:06:16,053
Either way, the IEA chief says the energy world will never be the same as it was before the war.

68
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:20,292
So what should we do? No panic. Just a plan.

69
00:06:20,292 --> 00:06:26,100
One: write down your household fuel costs for a month. You will see where you can cut.

70
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:31,512
Two: if you have a loan, work out in advance how much your EMI would change if rates rise.

71
00:06:31,512 --> 00:06:37,914
Three: many planners suggest keeping an emergency fund for three to six months of expenses.

72
00:06:37,914 --> 00:06:43,392
Four: avoid panic buying based on WhatsApp rumours like 'petrol will run out'.

73
00:06:43,392 --> 00:06:50,820
A sea lane far away is shaping our household budget. Those who understand it can prepare early.

74
00:06:50,967 --> 00:06:55,298
There is a free loan EMI calculator on calcsouq.com.

75
00:06:55,298 --> 00:06:58,717
What is the petrol price in your town today? Tell us in the comments.

76
00:06:58,717 --> 00:07:07,907
If this video helped, share it with your family, and subscribe to learn how world events affect your money.
